Cyclodextrin metal-organic frameworks (CD-MOF) are a class of biocompatible MOF with a great potential in drug delivery applications. Original CD-MOF crystals are fragile and large $(0.2\mathrm{-}1\ \mathrm{mm})$ , which are less useful in pharmaceutical applications. Cetyltrimethylammonium bromide and long chain poly(ethylene) glycol, used in size modulation to produce nanosized CD-MOF can compromise the biocompatibility, and physiochemical properties of CD-MOF as their complete removal from frameworks is difficult. To avoid the use of abovementioned modulators, herein, we demonstrate the synthesis of nanosized CD-MOF using triethylamine (TEA) as a modulator to reduce their size to ${\sim}254~\mathrm{nm}$ . The MOF characteristics such as crystal and chemical structure remain unaffected and the surface area of CD-MOF synthesised with TEA is measured $1075.5\mathrm{{m}}^{2}/\mathrm{{g}}$ almost $50\%$ higher than those of synthesised using bulky modulators. The improved CD-MOF architecture utilized for the insitu synthesis of silver nanoparticles resulted in enhanced antimicrobial efficacy tested against Staphylococcus aureus and Escherichia coli bacteria and Candida albicans fungus. And minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC) is recorded in the range of $31-15\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . Overall, the structural improvement in CD-MOF supported with thorough comparative investigations and enhanced antimicrobial efficacy could be very helpful in further establishing them in biomedicine field.
## 1. Introduction
Microbial-induced infectious diseases are a serious threat to human life and world economy causing millions of deaths each year world-wide (Larsson & Flach, 2022). After the discovery of Penicillin in 1928, the first antibiotic, thousands of drugs have been developed and antibiotics remain an indispensable approach to fight these dangerous microorganisms including bacteria, and fungi (Wenzel, 2020). However, mi­ crobes, especially bacteria started developing resistance against these antibiotics over the time due to misuse and overuse of these drugs, overgrowing population, poor sanitation and inappropriate sewage system (Fouz et al., 2020; Larson, 2007). Research has been ramped up to find alternatives to save human life and of all the antimicrobial ma­ terials such as metal-ion materials, organic materials, photocatalytic materials and natural compounds, nanoparticles-based approach has attracted great attention due to its high efficacy (Staron´ & Długosz, 2021). In particular, silver nanoparticles (AgNPs) possess a broad spectrum of antimicrobial activities due to their ability to penetrate trough microbial cell walls and interrupt cell deoxyribonucleic acid replication through release of sliver ions (Dakal et al., 2016). Apart from studying the mechanism of action, the effect of size, shape, charge, coating/capping, agglomeration and purity of AgNPs on the antimi­ crobial activity have been exploited extensively (Hong et al., 2016; Skandalis et al., 2017). The size of AgNPs was found to have a significant impact - smaller AgNPs exhibit higher antibacterial activity compared to the larger nanoparticles. It is concluded that AgNPs with size smaller than the $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ interacts well and could penetrate the cell wall more efficiently to reach the cytoplasm (site of action) (Agnihotri et al., 2014).
In addition to all of the excellent properties, one of the major drawbacks which is preventing AgNPs to be used as commercial antimicrobial materials is their poor underwater stability (Fernando & Zhou, 2019). Water-based nanoparticles suspensions tend to aggregate, resulting into loss of their antimicrobial functionality. The use of polymers, proteins and other stabilizing agents (surfactants) to prevent their aggregation is either inefficient or involves hazardous chemicals which compromise the biocompatibility of the formulation (Akter et al., 2018; AndrieuxLedier et al., 2013). AgNPs have been incorporated into polymer matrix, zeolites or metal-organic frameworks (MOF) for the stability enhance­ ment (Li et al., 2022; Rifai et al., 2006; Torres-Flores et al., 2021). MOF, a new class of porous materials have been exploited for their use in improving the stability of drugs and nanoparticles (Cao et al., 2021; Cure et al., 2019; Luzuriaga et al., 2019) in addition to other applica­ tions such as gas storage, imaging, sensing, catalysis due to their welldefined porosity and tunable size and structure (Lawson et al., 2021). Especially, cyclodextrin based MOF (CD-MOF) is the centre of attention in drug delivery research field due to: i) facile synthesis from edible components such as starch derived cyclic oligosaccharides (cyclodex­ trins), alkali metals (potassium hydroxide) and alcohols (ethanol), ii) dual cavities from cyclodextrin molecules $(0.7~\mathrm{nm})$ and framework ar­ chitecture $(1.7~\mathrm{nm})$ ) and iii) exceptional capability of cyclodextrins to form inclusion complex with the wide variety of molecules (He et al., 2019; Liu et al., 2016). Specifically, in healthcare, CD-MOF have been exploited for targeted drug delivery of anti-cancer drugs, dry powder inhalation, solubility enhancer and antibacterial materials (Huang et al., 2022; Shakya et al., 2019; Zhang et al., 2018). Several structure improvement studies have been also conducted to overcome the mois­ ture sensitive nature of CD-MOF such as by grafting cholesterol mole­ cules on the surface, incorporating fullerene (C60) into their cavities and forming hydrogel by directly cross-linking cyclodextrin units with ethylene glycol and diphenyl carbonate (Li et al., 2016; Singh et al., 2017a; Singh et al., 2017b).
In an interesting finding, nanocavities of CD-MOF have been utilized as nanoreactors for the synthesis of ultrafine AgNPs with a diameter of ${\sim}2{\rightarrow}\ \mathrm{nm}$ and their applicability as electric conductors has been demonstrated (Wei et al., 2012). Shakya et al., have shown the use of these AgNPs loaded CD-MOF as potential anti-bacterial material and the aqueous stability of nanoparticles was enhanced significantly after incorporation into the nanoporous framework (Shakya et al., 2019). The size of as synthesised CD-MOF is in the range of millimetre $(200\upmu\mathrm{m}$ to 1 mm) and not suitable for drug delivery applications due to their highly fragile and full-of-cracks architecture. Size modulation using acids, bases or surfactants is a popular method to improve the synthesis reproducibility and control the shape and size of MOF (Wang et al., 2022; Zahn et al., 2014). In all CD-MOF work mentioned above, their size was reduced to submicron to nanometer range by using cetyl­ trimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) and long chain poly(ethylene) glycol (mainly PEG 20,000) as modulators (Liu et al., 2016). However, both the modulators, CTAB and PEG with bulky molecular structure exhibit strong interaction with CD cavity and therefore, their complete removal from the highly cross-channel network is not possible even after extensive washing. It has been reported that $\upbeta$ -CD can form inclusion complex with CTAB molecules spontaneously and this can be mainly attributed to the hydrophobic interaction between the inner cavities of $\upbeta$ -CD and the alkyl chain of CTAB (Bagheri & Rafati, 2014). Likewise, interaction between $\upbeta$ -CD and PEG molecules has been also reported in aqueous solution via the formation of strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding (Valero et al., 2003). The presence of these bulky molecular chains could affect their physiochemical properties such as their surface area and pore blockage, ultimately affecting their drug/nanoparticles loading efficacy. Also, CTAB is a highly cytotoxic material with wellknown degradation effect on bio membranes. CTAB coated gold nano­ rods was found to be highly toxic on skin cells due to the residual CTAB molecules (Wang et al., 2008). Also, the cells viability test confirmed the significant cytotoxicity against human colon cancer cells $(65-75\ \%$ loss of viability) when it was exposed to CTAB-capped gold nanorods solu­ tion (Alkilany et al., 2009). Overall, use of these toxic and bulky mo­ lecular chains hinders the potential application of CD-MOF in biomedicine, and therefore, a safe alternative is required to utilise their excellent inherent biocompatible structure.
We envisage that the replacement of CTAB and PEG 20,000 with safe and low-molecular-weight modulator could help improving the physi­ cochemical properties of CD-MOF which will ultimately lead to the enhanced antimicrobial efficacy of silver loaded framework. After screening the list of modulators that could fit into our hypothesis, we observed that triethylamine (TEA), is the best alternative (Rodríguez et al., 2020; Usman et al., 2020) as it is a safe molecule which is used in some of the cosmetic products related to skin and hair (Fiume et al., 2013), and can be easily removed from the CD-MOF nanocavities due to its volatile nature and small molecular size (molecular weight, $101.19{\mathrm{g/}}$ mol). TEA has been used as a capping agent to stabilize the nanoparticles and its role to promote nucleation is well established in MOF (Li et al., 1999; Wang et al., 2018). The effect of TEA on the size reduction of AgNPs was also reported (Wu & Hsu, 2011). Herein, we report the sustainable synthesis and size modulation of CD-MOF by using TEA as a modulator for the first time. Furthermore, AgNPs were synthesised in­ side the nanocavities of newly synthesised CD-MOF and compared to those obtained using CTAB and PEG as modulators for its antimicrobial efficacy (tested against Staphylococcus aureus and Escherichia coli bac­ teria and Candida albicans fungus) and other important characteristics such as surface area and nanoparticles loading efficiency. Their anti­ microbial efficacy was assisted by the agar cut well method and minimal inhibitory concentration (MIC) herein.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials & regents
The $\gamma$ -cyclodextrin ( $\cdot\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD, $98~\%$ ) was purchased from Apollo Scien­ tific (UK). Regent grade, $90\%$ potassium hydroxide (KOH), molecular biology grade, $299\%$ cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB), poly (ethylene) glycol 20000 (PEG), HPLC grade absolute ethanol (EtOH), acetonitrile (anhydrous, $\geq98\ \%]$ , and HPLC grade, $299\ \%$ methanol (MeOH), $\geq99.5~\%$ triethyl-amine (TEA) and silver nitrate $\left({\tt A g N O3}\right)$ ACS reagent $(\geq99~\%)$ ) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (UK). For the antimicrobial tests, laboratory strain bacteria, S. aureus and $E$ . coli, and fungi C. albicans (ATCC 2091) were kindly provided by The University of Hertfordshire microorganism collection. Also, $25\ \%$ glutaraldehyde in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $4\%$ osmium tetroxide in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , nutrient agar, yeast peptone dextrose agar, nutrient broth, yeast peptone dextrose broth, Mueller Hinton broth and resazurin dye (Bioreagent grade) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (UK). $(\times10)$ phosphate buffered saline (Invitrogen (TM)) was obtained from Thermo Fisher Scientific (UK).
## 2.2. Synthesis of CD-MOF-TEA
CD-MOF nanoparticles were synthesised according to the previously reported methods (Liu et al., 2016; Singh et al., 2017b) with some modifications to make it more biofriendly. An aqueous solution $\mathrm{\Omega}_{\mathrm{\cdot}10\mathrm{mL}}$ of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ $324~\mathrm{mg}$ , $25~\mathrm{mM}$ and KOH ( $112\mathrm{mg}$ , $200~\mathrm{mM}.$ was prepared. Then, $1\mathrm{mL}$ of MeOH was directly added to the glass vial and heated for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then different amounts of TEA $\mathrm{(1~mL-3.0~mL)}$ ) was added followed by the addition of equal amount of MeOH as parent solution. The suspension was incubated at room temperature for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Finally, the precipitates were harvested using a centrifuge (5000 RPM) and repeatedly washed three times with EtOH and dried at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight under vacuum.
Control CD-MOF samples with CTAB and PEG and CD-MOF particles (without any modulator) were synthesised by replicating methods re­ ported previously (Liu et al., 2017) and the detailed synthesis procedure is presented in the supporting information. For clarity, CD-MOF synthesised using TEA, CTAB, or PEG is termed as CD-MOF-TEA, CDMOF-CTAB and CD-MOF-PEG, respectively. Large millimetre sized CDMOF particles have been obtained by slow introduction of MeOH va­ pours into aqueous solution of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ and KOH at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3–4 days (Smaldone et al., 2010).
## 2.3. In situ synthesis of AgNPs inside CD-MOF
For in-situ synthesis of AgNPs, CD-MOF crystals were first immersed in acetonitrile for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ (for solvent exchange) followed by replacement with $10\mathrm{mM}$ solution of ${\tt A g N O}_{3}$ in acetonitrile. Typically, $200\mathrm{mg}$ of CDMOF particles were immersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ acetonitrile solution of $10~\mathrm{mM}$ ${\tt A g N O}_{3}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The as-prepared Ag-CD-MOF particles were collected from the reaction solution using a centrifuge $(5000~\mathrm{RPM})$ , washed with acetonitrile several times and then left overnight for vacuum dry at $40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .
## 2.4. Characterizations
The samples were scanned for their morphologies using scanning electron microscope (SEM) on Zeiss XB1540 Crossbeam system. The sample powders were fixed on a metal stub using an adhesive carbon tape and then coated with a thin gold film before SEM examination at 5 kV voltage and $10~\mathrm{mA}$ current. The size of the nanoparticles was ana­ lysed using commercial ImageJ software. Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectra were recorded with a spectrophotometer (Spectrum Two™, Perkin Elmer) in the region of 600 to $4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the absorption mode.
Nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherm for CD-MOF samples were measured using a porosimeter (TriStar 3000 V6.05 A, USA). CD-MOFTEA, CD-MOF-CTAB, and CD-MOF-PEG were firstly activated by immersing in chloroform for three days and dried under vacuum at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ to remove the entrapped solvents. Specific amounts of samples (e.g. $150{\mathrm{-}}200\ \mathrm{mg})$ were loaded into the sample tubes and degassed under vacuum (10–5 Torr) at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ to remove any gas or solvent traces from the MOF cavities. Based on the gas adsorption efficiency $(\Nu_{2}$ in this case), specific surface area of the samples was calculated in area units per mass of sample $(\mathbf{m}^{2}/\mathbf{g})$ using Brunauer, Emmett and Teller (BET) model. The thermal properties of the samples based on weight change in relation to heating at constant temperature were characterised using Discovery TGA (TA Instruments, USA). For TGA, samples $(\sim5\mathrm{-}10$ mg) were placed in open aluminium pans and were heated from $40^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}500^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ with a ramp of $10^{\circ}\mathrm{C/min}$ . They were subsequently purged under a flux of nitrogen gas. The percentage of weight losses and onset thermal degradation temperatures were recorded from the TGA traces obtained.
The AgNPs embedded in CD-MOF samples were characterised using transmission electron microscopy (TEM) on JEOL 2100 200 KV system fitted with a LaB6 filament giving a point resolution of $0.13\mathrm{nm}$ operated on bright-field mode. The samples were dispersed in water to release the AgNPs and deposited into holey TEM copper grids. The morphology and size of the AgNPs were estimated using ImageJ software. To study the effect of modulators on the stability of AgNPs, samples in the form of suspension were stored at different temperatures, $4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for at least 1 month. The suspension was prepared by dissolving Ag-CDMOF in water at concentration of $1000\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . The stability was assisted by UV–Vis spectrometry (UV–Vis 3800). The UV–Vis spectrum were acquired in the $300{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}$ range and the distinct wavelength of UV or visible light absorbed by AgNPs in comparison to control was used to calculate the concentration of nanoparticles. A water sample without any Ag-CD-MOF was used as a control during the measurement. The UV spectra graphs were obtained using Origin Pro 2021 and the position of surface plasmon resonance (SPR) peaks were monitored. Zeta potential and size of the CD-MOF particles were measured using Malvern Pan­ alytical ZetaSizer (model Zen3600) using dynamic light scattering technique which operates on photon auto-correlation function. For zeta potential, powder samples were dispersed at concentrations of 0.1, 0.01 and $0.001~\mathrm{wt/v}\%$ in water and measured 3 times with 100 measure­ ments per time. For particle size, CD-MOF were dispersed in absolute ethanol at concentration of $1000~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ and measured 3 times with 15 measurements per time. Water without samples was used as a control.
## 2.5. Antimicrobial tests
To assess the antimicrobial activity of the CD-MOF crystals con­ taining silver nanoparticles, agar cut well method and MIC tests were performed. Laboratory strain bacteria, S. aureus and $E.$ coli, and fungi C. albicans (ATCC 2091) were grown in nutrient and yeast peptone dextrose broth, respectively.
## 2.5.1. Agar cut well method
Microbes were diluted to ${\sim}1{-}3\times10^{7}$ colony forming units (CFU/ mL) and inoculated onto Mueller Hinton agar plates. Wells $\mathbf{\dot{\nabla}}4\ \mathrm{mm}$ in diameter) were cut and sample suspensions at the concentration of 1000 $\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ were added. Plates were incubated and the diameter of the zone of inhibition was measured (in cm).
## 2.5.2. Minimal inhibitory concentration (MIC) tests
To investigate the MIC, sample suspensions were diluted to form a final concentration gradient from $500\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ to $7.8\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ . An antibiotic control was included to assess the antibiotic resistance of microbial strains. Then the plate was incubated with $\sim1{-}3\times10^{4}~\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ of microbes. Resazurin indicator dye was added and further incubated before assessing the colour change; the lowest concentration that did not change in colour was regarded as the MIC. An example of the plate template and resazurin colour change after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ is displayed in sup­ plementary Fig. S1. All incubation was done at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .
## 3. Results & discussion
## 3.1. Size modulation of CD-MOF using TEA
TEA is used for the first time to replace the bulky and toxic modu­ lators, CTAB and PEG 20,000 from synthesis of nanosized CD-MOF (Fig. 1). When observed under microscope, cracks were observed on the surface of large CD-MOF crystals synthesised (Fig. 2A). Since the introduction by Liu et al., in 2016, CTAB and PEG 20,000 have been used quite a lot to obtain nanosized particles of CD-MOF (He et al., 2021; Liu et al., 2016). In this study, we introduce safer modulator, TEA and the morphology of CD-MOF-TEA in comparison with CD-MOF-CTAB and CD-MOF-PEG samples is presented in Fig. 2B, C and D, respectively. A typical cubic shape could be observed in all samples with no significant difference between all the three modulators. However, it is important to note that the use of TEA has resulted in a faster solvothermal synthesis where crystallisation is completed in $^{1-2\mathrm{~h~}}$ compared to $6{-}12\mathrm{~h~}$ long process with other modulators and the synthesis time is in days when no modulator was used (Liu et al., 2016; Singh et al., 2017b; Smaldone et al., 2010). This could be attributed to the well-understood role of TEA to facilitate the crystallisation by enhancing deprotonation of organic ligands, cyclodextrin in this case. The deprotonation of hydroxyl functional groups of cyclodextrin using base has been confirmed pre­ viously and which might be the reason for faster synthesis of CD-MOF using TEA (Gaidamauskas et al., 2009).
## 3.2. In-situ synthesis of AgNPs inside CD-MOF
AgNPs synthesised in CD-MOF samples were detected under UV–Vis spectroscopy. The UV–Vis spectrum of AgNPs synthesised in CD-MOFTEA, CD-MOF-CTAB, CD-MOF-PEG and CD-MOF are shown in Fig. 3F. The wavenumber of the SPR peak can provide the information about the size of AgNPs, and the peak would shift towards lower wavenumber with decreasing AgNPs size (Paramelle et al., 2014). The SPR peaks for Ag-CD-MOF and Ag-CD-MOF-PEG were detected at $399~\mathrm{nm}$ while the SPR peak for Ag-CD-MOF-TEA was detected at slightly higher wave­ number of $405\mathrm{nm}$ . The SPR peak for Ag-CD-MOF-CTAB was detected at much higher wavenumber of $414~\mathrm{nm}$ . The obtained results suggested that the AgNPs released from CTAB modulated samples tend to aggre­ gate, might be due to the formation of inclusion complex between CTAB and the hydrophobic cavities of cyclodextrin units.
AgNPs loaded samples were subjected to thermal gravimetric anal­ ysis (TGA) to confirm the presence of polymers (surfactants) residue and investigate the amount of AgNPs inside the framework. TGA curves for CD-MOF-TEA and CD-MOF-CTAB showed identical thermal decompo­ sition behavior (Fig. 4A). The first thermal decomposition at $50^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}100^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ possibly represents the evaporation of the trapped solvents in the nanocavities of CD-MOF. The second decomposition at around ${175}^{\circ}\mathsf{C},$ could be mainly because of the degradation of $\gamma{\mathrm{-CD}}$ units (Smaldone et al., 2010). A similar trend was recorded for CD-MOF-PEG sample except for the decomposition of $\boldsymbol{\upgamma}$ -CD was delayed until around $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This could be explained by stabilisation affect from polymer chains on the MOF structure (Li et al., 2014) which confirmed the uncomplete removal of PEG chains from CD-MOF nanocavities. The TGA curve of CD-MOF-PEG presented another decomposition stage started at around $364^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which could be attributed to PEG degradation (Irfan Khan et al., 2015). Fig. 4A shows the TGA curves of silver loaded CD-MOF samples, Ag-CD-MOF, Ag-CD-MOF-TEA, Ag-CD-MOF-CTAB and AgCD-MOF-PEG. The overall thermal stability of all the four samples remained unaffected after the synthesis of AgNPs. The difference be­ tween the remaining weight of CD-MOF-TEA and Ag-CD-MOF-TEA recorded around $5\%$ which could be accounted for AgNPs. The weight $\%$ of AgNPs in $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ -CD-MOF-TEA recorded highest $(5.7~\%)$ when compared with the $4.4\%$ , $3.6~\%$ and $1\%$ in $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ -CD-MOF-PEG, Ag-CD
MOF-CTAB and Ag-CD-MOF, respectively (Fig. 4B). Additionally, the UV–Vis absorbance of AgNPs was compared from all three samples and as expected, highest absorbance intensity was recorded from $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ -CDMOF-TEA sample which again supported the claim that high surface area enhanced the AgNPs loading (Fig. 3F).
## 3.3. Stability of AgNPs synthesised inside CD-MOF particles
After successful synthesis of ultra-small AgNPs inside CD-MOF-TEA, the stability of the nanoparticles was assessed using Zeta potential and UV–Vis spectroscopy for 30 days of storage at $4^{\circ}\mathbf{C}_{:}$ , $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ As shown in Fig. 4C, an increase in the concentration of silver loaded crystals increased the zeta potential values, with $-51.37\pm1.64\mathrm{\mV},$ $-35.6\pm2.5\:\mathrm{mV}$ , $-28.5\pm0.5\:\mathrm{mV}$ and $-27.13\pm2.45\mathrm{mV}$ was recorded for Ag-CD-MOF, Ag-CD-MOF-TEA, Ag-CD-MOF-CTAB and Ag-CD-MOFPEG, respectively. Zeta potential of $-2.7\ \mathrm{mV}$ was measured for the deionized water (control). Zeta potential is the measurement of the electrostatic charge on the surface of particles; values outside the $\pm20{-}30~\mathrm{mV}$ range is regarded as stable due to the charged particles' ability to repel, thus unlikely to form agglomerates (Mourdikoudis et al., 2018). After dissolving MOF crystals in water, AgNPs released from CDMOF cavities would be stabilized by CD-units (Wei et al., 2012). The hydrophobic cavities of CD, which is well-known for its inclusion forming capabilities, forms host–guest interaction with nanoparticles and help them stabilize in the suspension (Huang et al., 2009). Ac­ cording to the obtained zeta potential values of $-35.6\ensuremath{\mathrm{mV}}$ , Ag-CD-MOFTEA can form the most stable suspension and AgNPs are unlikely to form agglomerates. The zeta potential of Ag-CD-MOF-PEG and Ag-CD-MOFCTAB is slightly closer to the instability range - might be due the competition between residual polymer chains and AgNPs to form in­ clusion complex with CD units. Nevertheless, the obtained data sug­ gested that all samples can form stable suspension in water. The zeta potential of Ag-CD-MOF-TEA suspension decreased slightly to $-31.6\pm$ $2.05\mathrm{mV}$ after storing it for 30 days at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 4D). Similar trend was observed in Ag-CD-MOF and Ag-CD-MOF-PEG suspensions.
## 3.4. Antimicrobial test results
The antimicrobial efficacy of Ag-CD-MOF-TEA was tested against bacteria, S. aureus and E. coli, and fungi C. albicans and compared with Ag-CD-MOF-PEG and Ag-CD-MOF-CTAB. Zone of inhibition diameters (ZOI) and overall MIC was used as the measurement standards. MIC and ZOI measured for different samples are presented as Fig. 5A and Table 1 respectively. Ag-CD-MOF-TEA has the highest antimicrobial efficacy in ZOI and showed excellent antifungal activity based on the obtained MIC results $(31-15~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ when compared to the other samples. The enhanced antimicrobial ability of the newly synthesised Ag-CD-MOF
TEA could be attributed to the improved physiochemical properties of CD-MOF, especially, the surface area which led to high AgNPs yield confirmed in above sections. However, the mechanism of action for $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ is not completely understood yet, but it is elucidated that ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ ions can i) puncture the cell wall by reacting with the peptidoglycan component, ii) inhibit the cellular respiration and disrupt the metabolic pathways through generation of reactive oxygen species and iii) also disrupt the DNA and its replication cycle ${\mathbf{A}}{\mathbf{g}}^{+}$ and their subsequent interaction and damage (Le Ouay & Stellacci, 2015).
The comparatively low antimicrobial efficacy of Ag-CD-MOF-CTAB samples could be due to the larger size of AgNPs as confirmed earlier. The large particles of Ag-CD-MOF showed no antimicrobial activity when tested using the cut well method, and required higher concen­ trations to have an effect, if any, when tested for their MIC. This could be due to two reasons, the inability of larger MOF particle size $(>100~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ to penetrate and slow frameworks degradation which might have affected the release of ${\bf A}{\bf g}^{+}$ ions. The second reason could be the less silver concentration inside CD-MOF, again due to slow diffusion of the solvent (containing silver precursor) inside crystals during in situ synthesis.
The growth kinetics of microbes treated with various concentrations of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ -CD-MOF-TEA was monitored over a $24\mathrm{~h~}$ period with an optical density reading taken every hour. As shown in Fig. 5 (B, C & D), the growth kinetics of the microbes, E. coli, S. aureus, and, fungi C. albicans was found concentration-dependent. Even at lower concentrations, AgCD-MOF-TEA was able to slightly reduce and delay the growth, while higher concentrations were able to completely inhibit the microbial growth. The results showed that $62~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ and higher concentration were able to inhibit $100\%$ growth of all three microbial when treated with $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ -CD-MOF-TEA. However, resazurin assay indicated that the MIC was higher against some of the microbes. A reason for the slight dif­ ference in results could be that the growth of microbes was inhibited by the Ag-CD-MOF-TEA, however the cells were still metabolically active, thus were able to reduce resazurin leading to a colour change. S. aureus is associated with two-thirds of the orthopedic implant infections and these infections are difficult to treat as the bacteria always tend to form biofilm (Ribeiro et al., 2012). Prashik et al., reported the MIC values of AgNPs - with an average size of $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ produced from nanoComposix company against S. aureus as $0.625\mathrm{mg/mL}$ which is very high compared to Ag-CD-MOF (Parvekar et al., 2020). Therefore, the AgNPs synthesised within CD-MOF matrix especially CD-MOF-TEA can provide a potent antimicrobial effect with very low concentration of AgNP as indicated by the MIC values.
## 4. Conclusion
In this research, we proposed TEA as a modulator to improve the physicochemical characteristics and hence, the antimicrobial efficiency of silver loaded CD-MOF by replacing conventional high-molecularweight PEG 20,000 and cytotoxic CTAB from the synthesis. The ob­ tained results show that TEA can be used an alternative for the rapid synthesis of uniform nanosized CD-MOF crystals. CD-MOF prepared using TEA as a modulator demonstrated higher specific surface area $(1075.5\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ compared to the samples synthesised with CTAB (455.12 $\mathbf{m}^{2}/\mathbf{g})$ and long chain PEG $(493.6~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ ). TEA modulated crystals used for synthesising ultra-small AgNPs has resulted in enhanced antimicro­ bial efficacy tested against both, bacteria and fungus. The improved CDMOF skeleton led to high silver nanoparticles payload, $58.3~\%$ , $29.5~\%$ and $470\%$ increase is measured when compared to CD-MOF synthesised without any modulator, with CTAB and PEG, respectively. Increment in ZOI diameters is also observed against all the tested microbes, specif­ ically, the MIC towards fungi C. albicans significantly enhanced (31–15 $\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}$ compared to those obtained from CTAB $\left(62{-}31|{\upmu}{\mathrm{g/mL}}\right)$ and PEG $(62{-}31\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL})$ ). Based on the above results, we strongly believe that this study could be very useful in further exploration of these newly syn­ thesised CD-MOF crystals in biomedicine field.
